@startuml
title Async module — operational errors and failure behavior
<style>
document {
  BackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  BoxPadding 10
  ParticipantPadding 10
}
title {
  FontSize 16
  FontColor #111111
}
</style>

actor "Caller" as Caller
participant "Async API" as Api
participant "Executor" as Executor
participant "Task promise" as Promise
participant "AsyncSharedState" as State
participant "Waiter coroutine" as Waiter

==  Expected-based operational errors ==
Caller -> Api : create / start / execute
Api -> Executor : execute(handle)
alt Invalid handle, stopped executor,\nqueue/allocation/startup failure
  Executor --> Api : std::unexpected(Error)
  Api --> Caller : Result<T> / Status error
else Operation accepted
  Executor --> Api : success
  Api --> Caller : success
end

==  Continuation scheduling failure ==
Promise -> State : complete task and signal waiter
State -> Executor : execute(waiter handle)
alt Continuation accepted
  Executor --> State : success
else Continuation rejected
  Executor --> State : unexpected(Error)
  State -> Waiter : resume inline fallback
  note right of Waiter
    This avoids leaving the waiter suspended, but
    can violate the requested executor affinity.
  end note
end

==  Unhandled coroutine exception ==
Promise -> Promise : unhandled_exception()
Promise -> Promise : std::terminate()
note over Promise
  Coroutine exceptions are not represented as Error
  in the current task result path.
end note

==  Scope join scheduling failure ==
Api -> Executor : schedule join continuation
Executor --> Api : unexpected(Error)
Api -> Api : std::terminate()

note over Api,Waiter
  Error is a module-local enum carried by std::expected.
  AsyncTask cancellation is distinct from operational Error.
end note
@enduml
